# a b s t r a c t

The adsorptive removal of diclofenac sodium (DCF), one of the most frequently detected pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs), was studied for the first time with metal–organic frameworks (MOFs). Zr-based MOFs, UiO-66 and functionalized UiO-66s (with \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)), were applied in the adsorption and the results were compared to that of activated carbon (AC). The pristine and functionalized UiO-66s showed much better performances than AC in terms of both the adsorption kinetics and capacities. The faster adsorption kinetics is significant as the pore size of UiO-66 is smaller than that of AC, which also suggests that certain interactions, such as electrostatic interaction or \(\pi{-}\pi\) stacking, might exist between DCF and UiO-66. In addition, the functionalization of UiO-66 with \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups resulted in a remarkable enhancement of the adsorption kinetics and capacity. However, the opposite trend was found after the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) functionalization of UiO-66. These results suggest that some additional interactions might exist, such as base–base repulsion between the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) group of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\)-UiO-66 and the NH group of DCF, or acid–base attraction between the \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) group of \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\)-UiO-66 and the NH group of DCF. Furthermore, at low concentrations, the adsorption capacity of the \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\)-functionalized UiO-66 was approximately 13-times higher than that of AC, which is very important from a practical point of view.

