# 1. Introduction

The use of pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) is increasing steadily because of the increasing world population, fast urbanization, and elevated living standards. The presence of

PPCPs is already detectable in surface water, as well as in ground water [1–3], and the bioaccumulation of these persistent PPCPs in the aquatic life will become a threat to the environment [4,5]. For example, the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency has already reported the presence of several PPCPs in the tissues of fishes collected from five rivers in the USA [6]. Diclofenac sodium (DCF, the structure of which is shown in Scheme 1) is one of the most common and representative PPCPs, which is significantly discharged into water resources and frequently found in drinking/surface water [7–10]. DCF is a very common, non-steroidal,
