Scheme 1. Chemical structure of DCF.

anti-inflammatory drug that is generally used to treat various inflammatory disorders [11], but chronic human exposure to DCF may cause detrimental effects, such as hemodynamic changes or thyroid tumors [12,13]. While a resolution for the acceptable limit of PPCPs in the environment or drinking water has not yet been established [14], the removal of these emerging pollutants from water resources is of vital concern to researchers worldwide.

To date, a number of procedures, such as photodegradation [15], coagulation–flocculation [16], biodegradation [17], chlorination [18], advanced oxidation processes (AOPs), and ozonation [19,20], have been used to remove PPCPs. The removal efficiency of AOPs and ozonation is good compared to that of other methods. However, in many cases, the use of AOPs or ozonation is energy intensive and expensive, and can produce residual toxic by-products [19,20]. On the other hand, adsorption-based techniques are drawing significant interest for removing PPCPs because of their simple operation and minimal energy consumption. Currently, activated carbons (ACs) are the most widely used adsorbents for the adsorption of PPCPs.

Over the last few decades, significant advancements have been achieved in the field of porous materials because of the development of new functional materials, including metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [21–29]. MOFs are receiving much attention because of their high porosity, with their pore size/shape being controllable over both microporous and mesoporous regions. In addition, the physicochemical properties of MOF materials can be tuned with easy modifications or functionalization, which ultimately increases their usability in various applications, such as adsorption/storage [30–32], separation [33], and catalysis [34]. For example, MOFs have recently been successfully used for fuel purification via adsorptive desulfurization [35–37] or denitrogenation [38–40]. MOFs have also been used for the aqueous-phase adsorption of different pollutants in water, such as heavy metals [41], phosphate [42], organics dyes [43–46], organic arsenic acids [47,48], bisphenol-A [49,50], and PPCPs (naproxen and clofibric acid) [51–53] from water. However, to the best of our knowledge, there have been no studies on the use of MOFs for the adsorptive removal of DCF from contaminated water. Moreover, the adsorptive removal of PPCPs from water with MOFs should be studied in detail because of high porosity and simple functionalization of MOFs. To date, the removal of DCF via adsorption has only been reported with different adsorbents such as AC, mesoporous silica, graphene oxide, and zeolites [4,54–57].

In this study, we investigated the aqueous-phase adsorption of DCF with MOFs to examine the potential of using MOFs as adsorbents for the removal of DCF. UiO-66 (UiO stands for University of Oslo) is a Zr-based MOF, which contains hexa-nuclear zirconium clusters linked by terephthalates [58]. It has received much attention because of its thermal and chemical stability [59], easy functionalization, and possible applications as an adsorbent [60,61] and in catalysis [62]. Both DCF and UiO-66 contain benzene rings, and therefore, there might be favorable interactions, such as

\(\pi{-}\pi\) interactions, between DCF and UiO-66 for adsorptive removal. Moreover, the adsorption of DCF by UiO-66 was also studied after functionalizing UiO-66 with acidic \(\mathrm{[SO_{3}H}\) , for \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -UiO-66) or basic \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) , for \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66) groups. The adsorption kinetics, adsorption isotherms, and the effects of the contact time and solution pH were also studied.

# 2. Experimental methods

# 2.1. Materials

All of the solvents and reagents were purchased from commercial vendors and used without further purification. Zirconium chloride \(\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4}}\) , \(99.5\%\) ), 1, 4-benzenedicarboxylic acid (terephthalic acid, \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) , \(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{6}\mathsf{O}_{4}\) , \(98.0\%\) ), and amino-terephthalic acid \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - BDC, \(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{N}_{0}\mathsf{4}\) , \(99.0\%\) ) were purchased from Sigma–Aldrich, and monosodium 2-sulfoterephthalic acid \(\mathrm{(NaSO_{3}H-B D C}\) , \(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{5}\mathsf{N a O}_{7}\mathsf{S}\) \(98\%\) ) was obtained from TCI Chemicals. Hydrochloric acid (HCl, \(36\%\) , ethanol \(\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{6}O,>99.5\%)}\) , acetic acid \(\mathrm{(C_{2}H_{4}O_{2},>99.5\%)}\) , acetone \(\mathrm{(C_{3}H_{6}O}\) , \(>99.5\%\) ) and N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, \(\mathsf{C}_{3}\mathsf{H}_{7}\mathsf{N O}\) , \(99.0\%\) ) were obtained from OCI Chemicals.

# 2.2. Synthesis of adsorbents

The synthesis of UiO-66 and \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66 was conducted according to the procedure reported by Cavka et al. [58], with a small modification. In brief, 2 mmol of \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) , 4 mmol of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) , 2 mmol of HCl, and \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF were added to a Teflon-lined autoclave (volume: \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) ) and heated in an electric oven for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C.\) For the synthesis of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66, the same molar amount of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC was used in replace of \(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BDC}\) . After the synthesis, the resulting solid was filtered and purified with DMF ( \(\,^{1}\,\mathrm{g}\) of the MOF in \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) of DMF and heated at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{5\,\mathrm{h}}\) ). After the purification process, the products were dried in a vacuum oven at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{12\,\mathrm{h}}\) . The synthesis of \(\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -UiO-66 was conducted according to the solvothermal synthetic route reported by Foo et al. [63]. In brief, 1.13 mmol of \(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}\) , 0.93 mmol of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) , and 0.20 mmol of \(\mathrm{NaSO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -BDC were added to a Teflon-lined autoclave (volume: \(100\,\mathrm{mL}\) ), and \(50\,\mathrm{mL}\) of a mixture of solvents (ratio of DMF to acetic acid \(=9{:}1\) (v/v)) was added. The mixture was stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) and heated at \(120\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(40\,\mathrm{h}\) in an electric oven. After cooling to room temperature, the resulting white solid was separated by centrifugation and washed three times with DMF. Finally, the sample was washed again with acetone and dried in a vacuum oven. Syntheses methods of other MOFs (MIL-101(Cr) and MIL100(Al, Ce, Fe)) were described in the supporting information.

# 2.3. Characterization

X-ray powder diffraction (XRD) analysis was conducted with a D2 Phaser (Bruker, Germany) using Cu Kα radiation. Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy was performed with a Jasco FTIR-4100, which can be used in the attenuated total reflection mode (maximum resolution: \(0.9\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) ). The surface area and pore size of the prepared samples were obtained from nitrogen adsorptions using a Tristar II 3020 (Micromeritics, USA). The nitrogen adsorptions were conducted at \(-196\,^{\circ}\!C\) and the samples were evacuated at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{12\,\mathrm{h}}\) before adsorption. The concentrations of DCF were measured with a UV spectrophotometer (UV-1800, Shimadzu, Japan).

# 2.4. Procedures for DCF adsorption

A DCF calibration curve was prepared by determining the absorbance at \(276\,\mathrm{nm}\) with a series of standard DCF solutions
\((1{-}50\,\mathrm{mg/L})\) at \({\mathsf{p H}}\,5.4\) , and the initial or equilibrium concentrations of DCF were calculated with the calibration curve. Prior to adsorption, the samples were dried for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) at \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) under vacuum conditions. For each batch, approximately \(5\;\mathrm{mg}\) of the sample was added to the DCF solution \(\mathrm{[50\,mL}\) , fixed concentration, \(\mathsf{p H}\ 5.4\\) and stirred for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) to \(^{12\,\mathrm{h}}\) at \(25\,^{\circ}\!C.\) After stirring the solution, the adsorbents were filtered with a syringe filter (PTFE, hydrophobic, \(0.5\;\upmu\mathrm{m}\) ) and the DCF concentration was determined from the absorbance of the UV spectrum. In the case of a high DCF concentration, the UV analysis was conducted after successive dilutions of the DCF solution. To determine the amount of adsorbed DCF, the following mass-balance relation (Eq. (1)) was used:

\[
q_{t}=(C_{0}-C_{t})\frac{V}{w}
\]

where \(C_{0}\,(\mathrm{mg/L})\) is the initial concentration, \(C_{t}\,(\mathrm{mg/L})\) is the concentration at time t, \(V\left(\mathrm{L}\right)\) is the volume of the DCF solution and \(w\left(\mathrm{g}\right)\) is the weight of the adsorbents. To determine the adsorption capacity at various condition of acidity, the pH of the DCF solution \((30\,\mathrm{mg/L})\) was adjusted with \(0.1\,\mathsf{M}\) aqueous solutions of HCl or NaOH.

# 2.5. Analysis of the adsorption data

The adsorption data were analyzed with a pseudo-second-order non-linear kinetic model, Langmuir isotherms, and separation factors \(\left(R_{L}\right)\) . Details of the methods used are given in the supporting information.

