# 3. Results and discussion

# 3.1. Characterization of the adsorbents

The XRD patterns of the synthesized MOFs (Fig. 1) show that the as-synthesized adsorbents have the UiO-66 structure since the patterns are identical to the simulated one. To prepare the functionalized UiO-66 samples, \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) - and \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -containing linkers \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC and \(\mathrm{NaSO}_{3}\) -BDC, respectively) were used in place of the simple \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) . In the case of \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -functionalization, a \(100\%\) replacement of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -BDC was achieved. However, in the case of the \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) functionalization, attempts to achieve more than an \(18\%\) replacement of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\) with \(\mathrm{NaSO}_{3}\) -BDC resulted in the formation of unstable UiO-66 structure [63]. From the XRD patterns in Fig. 1, it is clear that the structure of UiO-66 was not noticeably affected by \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) or \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) \((18\%)\) functionalization. Nevertheless, the nitrogen adsorption isotherms (Fig. 2a) reveal that the functionalization has decreased the porosity of UiO-66, with the porosities decreasing in the following order: \(\mathrm{UiO-66}>18\%\cdot\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H-UiO-}\) \(66>\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66, as shown in Table 1 and Fig. 2a. The extra volume of the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) moieties is probably responsible for the reduction in the porosity of the functionalized UiO-66 samples. The crystal structures of other MOFs were confirmed to be MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-100(Al, Cr, Fe) since the XRD patterns of the synthesized MOFs matched well with the simulated patterns as shown in supporting Fig. S1. Again, the nitrogen adsorption isotherms of MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-100(Al, Cr, Fe) were shown in supporting Fig. S2 and the textural properties were also summarized in Table S1.

The functionalization of UiO-66 with \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups was also verified with FTIR spectra (Fig. 2b). In the FTIR spectrum for \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -UiO-66, a \(\mathsf{C}\mathrm{{-}\mathsf{N}}\) stretching band and \(\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}\) wagging band are present at 1258 and \(764\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , respectively, which confirms the successful incorporation of the \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) groups [60]. On the other hand, large changes in the FTIR spectrum of the \(18\%\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -UiO-66 are evident because of the additional \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups. The bands present at 1237 and \(1180\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) may be due to \(\scriptstyle0=S=0\) symmetric and asymmetric stretching modes, respectively [34]. The band centered on \(1080\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is due to \(\scriptstyle S=0\) stretching vibrations and the band for \(S0_{3}\) is present at \(1020\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . The \(S0_{3}\) band has slightly shifted from \(1030\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which is probably due to the presence of the \(S0_{3}\) -substituted aromatic rings [34,64].

