MIL-100s are well-known MOFs that have high surface areas (see supporting Table S1), and they have shown very good adsorption performances with other PPCPs [51] and does [43]. However, the DCF adsorption performance of the MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-100s MOFs is poor compared to that of UiO-66. Even though it is not easy to explain why other MOFs show the low amounts of adsorbed DCF, relatively low zeta potential or negative surface charge of the MOFs (for example, MIL-100s [46,47]) may be one reason considering the electrostatic interaction between UiO-66 and DCF (see Section 3.4). Further work is needed to understand why other MOFs exhibit poor results for the adsorption of DCF. In this work, the DCF adsorption performance of the promising UiO-66 samples (virgin and functionalized ones), as well as AC, was studied in detail.

To understand the adsorptive performances of the samples, the effects of contact time on the adsorption of DCF with UiO-66, \(18\%\) \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) -UiO-66, \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) -UiO-66, and AC were evaluated, with the adsorption results shown in Fig. 4. At the beginning of the adsorption tests, rapid adsorption occurs with all of the adsorbents because of the large number of vacant surface sites. As the adsorption time increases, the available sites become saturated, and ultimately, equilibrium is achieved. As shown in Fig. 4, for the whole adsorption time, the adsorbed quantity of DCF decreases in the following order: \(18\%\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66>\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66>\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{UiO}\mathrm{-}66>\mathrm{AC}\) . Fig. 4 also shows that the adsorption of DCF was almost completed after \(6\,\mathrm{{h}}\) with the virgin and functionalized UiO-66 samples, whereas it took approximately \(12~\mathrm{h}\) with AC. However, the adsorption time was extended to \(24~\mathrm{h}\) to achieve equilibrium.

