To compare the adsorption kinetics precisely, a pseudo-second-order non-linear kinetic model [65] was adopted. The solid lines in Fig. 4 show the non-linear plots of the pseudo-second-order rate equations, and the pseudo-second-order rate constants \(\left(k_{2}\right)\) are listed in Table 1. The high correlation factor \((r^{2})\) confirms that the pseudo-second-order model fits the experimental results with a high degree of accuracy for the adsorptions in the studied concentrations. The \(k_{2}\) values for the adsorption of DCF with UiO-66 is 3.5-times higher than that of the conventional AC, and this rapid adsorption of DCF by UiO-66 is significant because the pore size of UiO-66 \((0.5{-}0.7\ \mathrm{nm})\) [61,66] is much smaller than that of AC \((3{-}4\;\mathrm{nm})\) (see supporting Fig. S4). Therefore, it may be presumed that the rapid adsorption of DCF by UiO-66 may be due to the presence of specific interactions between the UiO-66 and DCF (see Section 3.4). In addition, after functionalizing the UiO-66 with \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}\) groups, \(k_{2}\) value has increased, whereas the reverse effect is observed when UiO-66 is functionalized with \(\mathsf{N H}_{2}\) groups. Hence, apart from a specific interaction between UiO-66 and DCF, the acid and base functionalities also appear to play a vital role during the adsorption of DCF with UiO-66.

# 3.3. Adsorption isotherm

After allowing adsorption to occur for an adequately period, i.e., \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) , isotherms were obtained for the adsorption of DCF with the four adsorbents (virgin UiO-66, \(S0_{3}\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) functionalized UiO-66s, and AC), and the results are shown in Fig. 5. The virgin UiO-66 can adsorb a much higher amount of DCF than AC under the same experimental conditions, which proves the potential of the MOF for the adsorption of DCF. To determine the maximum adsorption capacity \((Q_{0})\) , Langmuir plots [67] (Fig. 6a) were used, and the \(Q_{0}\) values are summarized in Table 1. The table shows that the \(Q_{0}\) value of the virgin UiO-66 is \(189\;\mathrm{mg/g}\) , which is
